{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 4077145f (resume after a definitive refusal in the M1 install).\n\nNo. Resume covers unknown outcomes, not definitive refusals. In c643756e, \"visible and resumable\" means that a subordinate step whose outcome is unknown (a restart, a lost reply, an interrupted phase) is continued from its recorded phase under the one signed install, by the existing outbox and recovery rules, until that step has a recorded result; the install then completes or is refused exactly once. A definitive subordinate refusal is a terminal result for that install act: the install's state becomes refused, the record shows which step refused and why, no grants are added and no application is born, and the refused message stays permanently idempotent as native deliveries already are. There is no automatic retry policy and no resume signal. When conditions change, for example the operator adds the release to the trust list, the admin signs a new install act. That is the ordinary next act, not a second signature invented for the old one; the prohibition in c643756e was on the host manufacturing authority on the admin's behalf. A new install act after a refused one is admitted on its own terms, and the record shows both.\n\nConfirmed as you propose: one new rules version per install, one activation of the entry's validated closure, directory-owned checked forwarding to membership and creation, the exact original installer and the default roles named in the install act, finite phase and request bounds, and no cross-scope transaction. Record the phases so that a reader can tell pending from refused from active without live state, and say in the install half's implementation note that a refused install is final."}